# This assembly code (unlike MIPS) assumes word-addressable memory 
  lw $s3, 1($0)     # read memory word 1 into $s3

The lw instruction specifies the effective address in memory as thesum of a base address and an offset . The base address (written in parentheses in the instruction) is a register. The offset is a constant (written before the parentheses). The base address is $0 , which holds the value 0, and the offset is 1, so the lw instruction reads from memory address ( $0+1 ) 1 . After the load word instruction ( lw ) is executed, $s3 holds the value 0xF2F1AC07, which is the data value stored at memory address 1.

# This assembly code (unlike MIPS) assumes word-addressable memory 
  sw $s7, 5($0)  # write $s7 to memory word 5

Code writes the contents of register $s7 into memory word 5. These examples have used $0 as the base address for simplicity, but remember that any register can be used to supply the base address.


The MIPS assembly code reads words 0, 2, and 3 and writes words 1, 8, and 100. The offset can be written in decimal or hexadecimal.
lw $s0, 0($0)      # read data word 0 (0xABCDEF78) into $s0
lw $s1, 8($0)      # read data word 2 (0x01EE2842) into $s1
lw $s2, 0xC($0)    # read data word 3 (0x40F30788) into $s2
sw $s3, 4($0)      # write $s3 to data word 1
sw $s4, 0x20($0)   # write $s4 to data word 8
sw $s5, 400($0)    # write $s5 to data word 100


# $s0 = a, $s1 = b
  addi $s0, $s0, 4       # a = a + 4
  addi $s1, $s0, -12     # b = a - 12


